.Dd 2025-09-30
.Dt SSL_HANDLE_EVENTS 3ossl
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm SSL_handle_events
.Nd advance asynchronous state machine and perform network I/O
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In openssl/ssl.h
.Ft int
.Fo SSL_handle_events
.Fa "SSL *ssl"
.Fc
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Fn SSL_handle_events
performs any internal processing which is due on an SSL object.
The exact operations performed by
.Fn SSL_handle_events
vary depending on what kind of protocol is being used with the given SSL
object.
For example,
.Fn SSL_handle_events
may handle timeout events which have become due, or may attempt, to the
extent currently possible, to perform network I/O operations on one of
the BIOs underlying the SSL object.
.Pp
The primary use case for
.Fn SSL_handle_events
is to allow an application which uses OpenSSL in nonblocking mode to
give OpenSSL an opportunity to handle timer events, or to respond to the
availability of new data to be read from an underlying BIO, or to
respond to the opportunity to write pending data to an underlying BIO.
.Pp
.Fn SSL_handle_events
can be used only with the following types of SSL object:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It DTLS SSL objects
Using
.Fn SSL_handle_events
on an SSL object being used with a DTLS method allows timeout events to
be handled properly.
This is equivalent to a call to
.Xr DTLSv1_handle_timeout 3 .
Since
.Fn SSL_handle_events
handles a superset of the use cases of
.Xr DTLSv1_handle_timeout 3 ,
it should be preferred for new applications which do not require support
for OpenSSL 3.1 or older.
.Pp
When using DTLS, an application must call
.Fn SSL_handle_events
as indicated by calls to
.Xr SSL_get_event_timeout 3 ;
event handling is not performed automatically by calls to other SSL
functions such as
.Xr SSL_read 3
or
.Xr SSL_write 3 .
Note that this is different to QUIC which also performs event handling
implicitly; see below.
.It QUIC connection SSL objects
Using
.Fn SSL_handle_events
on an SSL object which represents a QUIC connection allows timeout
events to be handled properly, as well as incoming network data to be
processed, and queued outgoing network data to be written, if the
underlying BIO has the capacity to accept it.
.Pp
Ordinarily, when an application uses an SSL object in blocking mode, it
does not need to call
.Fn SSL_handle_events
because OpenSSL performs ticking internally on an automatic basis.
However, if an application uses a QUIC connection in nonblocking mode,
it must at a minimum ensure that
.Fn SSL_handle_events
is called periodically to allow timeout events to be handled.
An application can find out when it next needs to call
.Fn SSL_handle_events
for this purpose (if at all) by calling
.Xr SSL_get_event_timeout 3 .
.Pp
Calling
.Fn SSL_handle_events
on a QUIC connection SSL object being used in blocking mode is not
necessary unless no I/O calls (such as
.Xr SSL_read 3
or
.Xr SSL_write 3 )
will be made to the object for a substantial period of time.
So long as at least one call to the SSL object is blocking, no such call
is needed.
However,
.Fn SSL_handle_events
may optionally be used on a QUIC connection object if desired.
.Pp
With the thread-assisted mode of operation
.Xr OSSL_QUIC_client_thread_method 3
it is unnecessary to call
.Fn SSL_handle_events
as the assist thread handles the QUIC connection events.
.El
.Pp
Calling
.Fn SSL_handle_events
on any other kind of SSL object is a no-op.
This is considered a success case.
.Pp
Note that
.Fn SSL_handle_events
supersedes the older
.Xr DTLSv1_handle_timeout 3
function for all use cases.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
Returns 1 on success and 0 on failure.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr SSL_get_event_timeout 3 ,
.Xr DTLSv1_handle_timeout 3 ,
.Xr ssl 7
.Sh HISTORY
The
.Fn SSL_handle_events
function was added in OpenSSL 3.2.
